# GranoAndClick Evolution

This repository is a personal evolution of the original E-commerce project created by Tam 1 CH61. My goal with this fork was to take a functional codebase and apply professional architecture and DevOps standards to learn how real-world applications are scaled and maintained.

## Refactoring Goals

As a developing engineer, I wanted to move beyond basic procedural JavaScript. My main focus was applying SOLID principles and improving the developer experience through containerization.

### Architecture and SOLID Principles

While I am still deepening my understanding of these patterns, I restructured the Vanilla JS logic using the following criteria:

* Single Responsibility (SRP): I worked on separating the functions that handle price calculations from those that manipulate the DOM. The cart logic is now more independent and easier to track.
* Dependency Injection: I started using abstractions so that UI components don't rely directly on global functions, which allowed me to implement unit testing with Vitest.
* Maintainability: My objective was to transform the JS into a modular system that is more readable and easier for other developers to follow.

### Infrastructure with Docker (GranoAndClick Architecture)

To eliminate "it works on my machine" issues, I configured a development environment based on Docker using a Multistage approach:

* Development Stage (Debian Bookworm): I used a Node 22 image to access modern testing tools like Vitest and Playwright. I configured the user with UID 1000 to ensure seamless Git operations from my Fedora host without permission conflicts.
* Production Stage (Nginx Alpine): I created an optimized final stage with Nginx to demonstrate how the project would be efficiently served in a real production environment.
* Host-Container Sync: The code resides on the host and is mounted into the container. I configured Nginx with "sendfile off" so that any changes I make to CSS or HTML are reflected instantly without being blocked by server caching.

## Getting Started

This environment is specifically designed for VS Code Dev Containers.

1. Preparation:
Ensure the setup script has execution permissions on your local system:
chmod +x .devcontainer/setup.sh
2. Launching the Environment:
When you open this folder in VS Code, the editor will detect the configuration and prompt you to "Reopen in Container." This will automatically install Playwright dependencies and spin up a MariaDB 11.2 database.
3. Testing:
I have included initial tests to ensure my refactoring doesn't break original functionality:

* Unit Tests: npm run test:unit
* E2E Tests: npm run test:e2e

## Learning Notes

This project is an ongoing work in progress. I recognize there is still much to learn regarding state management and Docker image optimization, but this refactor represents my current progress in applying software architecture and automation best practices.

